                    <entry>2. ________</entry>
                    <entry>7. ________</entry>
                    <entry>12. ________</entry>
                    <entry>17. ________</entry>
                    <entry>22. ________</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>3. ________</entry>
                    <entry>8. ________</entry>
                    <entry>13. ________</entry>
                    <entry>18. ________</entry>
                    <entry>23. ________</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>4. ________</entry>
                    <entry>9. ________</entry>
                    <entry>14. ________</entry>
                    <entry>19. ________</entry>
                    <entry>24. ________</entry>
                  </row>
                  <row>
                    <entry>5. ________</entry>
                    <entry>10. ________</entry>
                    <entry>15. ________</entry>
                    <entry>20. ________</entry>
                    <entry>25. ________</entry>
                  </row>
                </tbody>
              </tgroup>
            </table>
          </item>
        </list>
      </note>
    </section>
    <section class="excel-labs" id="sect-00008">
      <note id="fs-idm27544368" class="statistics collab">
        <label/>
        <para id="eip-34">In your classroom, try this exercise. Have class members write down the average time (in hours, to the nearest half-hour) they sleep per night. Your instructor will record the data. Then create a simple graph (called a <emphasis>dot plot</emphasis>) of the data. A dot plot consists of a number line and dots (or points) positioned above the number line. For example, consider the following data:</para>
        <para id="eip-idm80945824">
          <list id="eip-909" list-type="labeled-item" display="inline">
            <item>5</item>
            <item>5.5</item>
            <item>6</item>
            <item>6</item>
            <item>6</item>
            <item>6.5</item>
            <item>6.5</item>
            <item>6.5</item>
            <item>6.5</item>
            <item>7</item>
            <item>7</item>
            <item>8</item>
            <item>8</item>
            <item>9</item>
          </list>
        </para>
        <para id="eip-71">The dot plot for this data would be as follows:</para>
        <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idp25549888">
          <media id="id44761709a" alt="This is a dot plot showing average hours of sleep. The number line is marked in intervals of 1 from 5 to 9. Dots above the line show 1 person reporting 5 hours, 1 with 5.5, 3 with 6, 4 with 6.5, 2 with 7, 2 with 8, and 1 with 9 hours." longdesc="m16020_DotPlot_description.html">
            <image src="../../media/fig-ch01_02_01.png" mime-type="image/png"><param name="longdesc" value="m16020_DotPlot_description.html"/></image>
          </media>
        </figure>
        <para id="id10326643">Does your dot plot look the same as or different from the example? Why? If you did the same example in an English class with the same number of students, do you think the results would be the same? Why or why not?</para>
        <para id="id9336363">Where do your data appear to cluster? How might you interpret the clustering?</para>
        <para id="id9969457">The questions above ask you to analyze and interpret your data. With this example, you have begun your study of statistics.</para>
      </note>
      <para id="para-00011">The Excel labs below are designed to accompany this course. The first Excel lab introduces the <emphasis>basic skills needed to use Excel</emphasis>. Later labs will align with examples from the textbook and will help you use Excel to perform statistical calculations and analyze data.</para>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <title>Notes on Using These Files</title>
        <item>These files work best using the <emphasis>desktop version of Microsoft Excel</emphasis>, ideally on a <emphasis>Windows computer</emphasis>.</item>
        <item>They may not function the same way in the <emphasis>cloud (online) version of Excel</emphasis>, and some features may behave slightly differently in <emphasis>Google Sheets</emphasis>. While most steps should still work, you may notice small differences in formulas, menus, or formatting.</item>
      </list>
      <note id="note-00003">
        <title>Download Excel Labs</title>
        <link url="https://openstax.org/r/excel-lab-intro/">Excel 101 - Intro to Using Excel</link>
        <newline/>
        <link url="https://openstax.org/r/excel-lab-1.3">Excel Lab 1.3</link>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
  <glossary><definition id="fs-idm109278432">
      <term>Explanatory Variable</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm31399376">the independent variable in an experiment; the value controlled by researchers</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm58845216">
      <term>Treatments</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm22575600">different values or components of the explanatory variable applied in an experiment</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm22025168">
      <term>Response Variable</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm21518528">the dependent variable in an experiment; the value that is measured for change at the end of an experiment</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm37719200">
      <term>Experimental Unit</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm24214112">any individual or object to be measured</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idp2339632">
      <term>Lurking Variable</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm53118336">a variable that has an effect on a study even though it is neither an explanatory variable nor a response variable</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="def-00001">
      <term>Randomized Experiment</term>
      <meaning>a study in which the researcher manipulates values of the explanatory variable by assigning experimental units to treatments at random and measures the resulting changes in the response variable; a properly designed randomized experiment can demonstrate a cause-and-effect connection</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm60242624">
      <term>Random Assignment</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm36285312">the act of organizing experimental units into treatment groups using random methods</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm44014688">
      <term>Control Group</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idp2890064">a group in a randomized experiment that receives an inactive treatment but is otherwise managed exactly as the other groups</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm53331472">
      <term>Informed Consent</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idp6209616">Any human subject in a research study must be cognizant of any risks or costs associated with the study. The subject has the right to know the nature of the treatments included in the study, their potential risks, and their potential benefits. Consent must be given freely by an informed, fit participant.</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm1693648">
      <term>Institutional Review Board</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idp35054208">a committee tasked with oversight of research programs that involve human subjects</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm46313024">
      <term>Placebo</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm28719952">an inactive treatment that cannot directly affect the response variable, used to counter the power of suggestion</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm26631824">
      <term>Blinding</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm40749296">not telling participants which treatment they are receiving</meaning>
    </definition><definition id="fs-idm21639776">
      <term>Double-Blind Experiment</term>
      <meaning id="fs-idm25589376">an experiment in which both the subjects of an experiment and the researchers who work with the subjects are blinded</meaning>
    </definition></glossary>
</document>